# GitHub source control — branches, PRs, review

Source-control operations against GitHub — **create branch, open pull request,
request review, leave review comment, merge pull request** — go through the
**GitHub MCP server**. Any practice skill that needs to branch code, ship a
change for review, or touch a PR routes through here.

The GitHub MCP server is **account-wide** — OAuth'd to you, it works across every
repository you can access. It is *not* pinned to one repo.

## Which repository

This capability is not tied to a single repo. The target repo is **whichever one
the work is in** — resolve it exactly as the workspace rule does: from
`.workspace.agents.json` (match the repo by `name`/`path`/`remote`/`owner`), or
the repo you're already operating inside. For a change that spans repos, treat
each repo's PR as a separate unit of work.

## Access

All owned operations go through the GitHub MCP server. If it's unreachable or
unauthenticated, **halt and report** — do not fall back to the `gh` CLI or the
raw REST API for these operations.

First operation of a session: confirm the MCP server is reachable, and that
you've identified the correct target repo (`owner/name`) from the workspace map.

## Conventions

### Branch naming
- `feature/{WORK_ITEM_NUMBER}` — features and feature-like work.
- `bugfix/{WORK_ITEM_NUMBER}` — bug fixes.
- `patch/{short-kebab-slug}` — small patches with no backing work item.

### Worktrees — isolate each branch in its own working directory
When kicking off work, create the branch in a **dedicated git worktree** rather
than switching the main checkout. Each worktree has its own working tree, index,
and `HEAD` backed by the shared `.git`, so parallel efforts — including parallel
agents — never clobber each other's files or build state.

- **Default for `feature/`/`bugfix/` work.** A trivial `patch/`-class change or a
  one-line fix may stay in the main checkout. The worktree earns its keep once
  work runs in parallel or spans more than a quick edit.
- **Location.** One worktree per branch under a gitignored `.worktrees/` dir at
  the repo root: `.worktrees/<branch>` (e.g. `.worktrees/feature/42`). Ensure
  `.worktrees/` is in `.gitignore`.
- **Create.** `git worktree add -b <branch> .worktrees/<branch>` for a new branch,
  or `git worktree add .worktrees/<branch> <branch>` for an existing one. The
  local branch is created with the worktree and published on first push. The
  pull request, review, and merge still route through the GitHub MCP server.
- **Cost is real.** Untracked files, `node_modules`, and build artifacts are
  **not** shared from the main checkout — install dependencies in the new
  worktree before building, and remember that environment-shared services (e.g.
  the single local QA stack) are contended, not duplicated.
- **Clean up after merge.** Once the PR merges, `git worktree remove
  .worktrees/<branch>` and delete the branch. Don't leave stale worktrees behind.

### Pull request body
Every PR body must include:
- **Summary** of the change.
- **How it satisfies the acceptance criteria** (when a work item is linked).
- **Test evidence** — what was run, what passed, what was added.
- **Risks and follow-ups.**
- **Link to the backing work item** (e.g. `Fixes #123`), except for `patch/` branches.

### Review and merge
- Request review per the target repo's CODEOWNERS or stated policy.
- Use that repo's standard merge strategy (squash, rebase, or merge commit) — don't deviate without user confirmation.

## Composition

When a work-item tracker is installed (e.g. `github-projects`), link pull
requests back to work items via the work-item-number reference in the branch
name and PR body. Notifying the tracker of PR state is the tracker's job, not
this rule's — this ends at the PR.
